  "generated_text": "1. Each die has 6 faces, numbered from 1 to 6.\n2. The product of two numbers is even if at least one of the numbers is even.\n3. The even numbers on a six-sided die are 2, 4, and 6.\n4. There are 3 even numbers on each die.\n5. The probability of rolling an even number on one die is \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n6. The probability of rolling an odd number on one die is \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n7. The probability that both dice show odd numbers is \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}\\).\n8. The probability that at least one die shows an even number is the complement of the probability that both dice show odd numbers.\n9. Therefore, the probability that at least one die shows an even number is \\(1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}\\).\n\nSo, the probability that the product of the numbers rolled is even is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[3/4, '\\\\frac{3}{4}']",
    "gold_answer": "There are $6 \\times 6 = 36$ possible outcomes.  The only way that they can roll an odd product is if both their rolls are odd.  Since 3 of the 6 faces on each die are odd, this can occur in $3 \\times 3 = 9$ ways.  So a even product can occur in $36-9= 27$ ways, and the probability is thus $\\dfrac{27}{36} = \\boxed{\\dfrac34}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
